!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a schema-layer sidecar (tests/class2/schema/**/*-expected.tn) -- [TSON-SCHEMA]
  §5's declaration forms and §8's resolved output. See README.md's "The Class 2 layers" and
  RUNNER.md.

  This is the first layer of Class 2, and the one that needs no invented expectation format. §1.3
  makes producing a resolved schema value a MUST and §8 fixes its serialization, so a vector's
  subject is a schema document and its expected side is the resolved output that document denotes,
  written in the spec's own §8 form.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A resolved schema, as §8.1's ingest form: a `!schema` map of `name => !type_definition { ... }`,
    exactly what `spec/m/*-resolved.tn` carry and what §8.2 keys identity on.

    Carried as a multi-line token rather than modelled field by field, which would restate meta.tn
    inside this schema and leave two statements of one shape to drift apart. A runner reads the text
    with its own meta.tn-governed reader -- so the payload is validated against the spec's own
    schema, by the implementation under test, rather than against a paraphrase of it here. Nothing
    is escaped on the way: a multi-line token is raw text, which is why this stays inside the
    conservative sidecar subset RUNNER.md rule 2 states.

    The `subtypes` index §8.2 calls resolver-managed is part of that output and is stated where a vector
    has one: the layer is about what a schema document resolves to, and an entry that another entry
    composes with says so here. What §2.2.3's import closure makes of a *namespace* is the link layer's,
    which is why nothing at this layer states an imported name.

    No subject at this layer declares a template, and that is a boundary rather than an omission.
    §8.1 says an **open** entry is serialized as its declaration -- `<params> !C core-value`, the held
    body -- rather than as a `type_definition` value. But the kernel declares the resolved document's own
    type as `schema => {type_name => type_definition}`, so a document carrying one does not conform to
    the schema this layer's expected side is read against, and no runner can read it. What a template
    resolves to is stated at the link layer instead, over the entries it mints.

    The resolved document carries no header. Which schema governs it is not the vector's to say --
    it is meta.tn for every vector at this layer -- and a runner names it when it reads.
    """
  schema_valid => {
    resolved: text
  }

  @doc:"""
    An `error` vector's category is `resolver` throughout this layer. [TSON-DATA] §8.1 says so
    outright for Part 2: "every error that makes a schema fail to load or ingest -- incoherent
    constraint values, invalid defaults, refuted assertions, failed ingest checks -- is a resolver
    error, however value-like the violated rule, because it is detected while resolving the schema.
    Validation errors are reserved for data checked against a successfully loaded schema."

    So a bound that cannot be met and a default of the wrong type are both `resolver` here, and
    `validation` at this layer would be wrong however much the rule reads like a constraint.
    """
  schema_sidecar => sidecar_common & spliced_header & {
    ( valid: schema_valid | error: vector_error )
  }
}
